# XCU timeout
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The utilities run under timeout are sh -c command strings that loop on
# sleep 1 (so a signal is handled within a second) or ignore a signal.
# Time limits are one second; every call that can only end by the limit is
# bounded by the limit, or by -k for a utility that ignores SIGTERM, and
# the driver's own timeout stands behind them.

assertion  XCU.timeout.001
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      2024
says       When the utility ends within the time, timeout exits with its
           status, passes its output on, and gives it the arguments.
test       t/001-in-time.sh

assertion  XCU.timeout.002
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      2024
says       When the limit is reached the utility is sent SIGTERM and
           timeout exits 124.
test       t/002-limit-reached.sh

assertion  XCU.timeout.003
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      2024
says       timeout -s signal_name sends that signal instead of SIGTERM,
           names being accepted in lower case and without SIG; the exit
           status is 124.
test       t/003-s-signal.sh

assertion  XCU.timeout.004
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      2024
says       timeout -p, when the limit is reached, exits with the status of
           the utility instead of 124; without -p the same run gives 124.
test       t/004-p-preserve.sh

assertion  XCU.timeout.005
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       timeout -k time sends SIGKILL to a utility that still runs after
           time has passed since the first signal, so a utility that
           ignores SIGTERM is ended; the exit status is 124 or the status
           of the process ended by SIGKILL.
test       t/005-k-kill.sh

assertion  XCU.timeout.006
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      2024
says       timeout exits 127 when the utility cannot be found, 126 when it
           is found but cannot be run, and 125 for an error of its own (a
           duration that is not a number).
test       t/006-errors.sh

assertion  XCU.timeout.007
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      2024
says       The duration may have a fraction and the suffixes s, m, h and d;
           0.5s stops a long-running utility and 1m, 1h and 1d leave a
           short one alone.
test       t/007-duration-forms.sh

assertion  XCU.timeout.008
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      2024
says       A duration of zero sets no limit: the utility runs to its end
           and its status is passed on.
test       t/008-zero.sh

assertion  XCU.timeout.009
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      2024
says       When the utility ends by a signal other than from the time limit,
           timeout ends itself by the same signal.
test       t/009-signal-propagation.sh

assertion  XCU.timeout.010
anchor     utilities/timeout.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      2024
says       timeout -f is accepted and the limit still stops the utility
           (exit 124).
test       t/010-f.sh
